=== skills/workstation/delegate/SKILL.md ## Choose by tier
## Choose by tier

A LiteLLM tier is the unit you plan with: you name the tier and LiteLLM walks its chain, so you never pick the
model. Read the tier names and chains in `registry/litellm-tiers.json`; the gateway runs one with
`model-gateway run --tier <tier> --json '{...}'` (a tier is not combined with `client`, `model`, `profile` or
`harness`; an unknown tier is `unknown-tier`; `batch` takes `client` and `model`, so a tier delegation is a
`run`).

| Work | Tier |
|---|---|
| Public reading and trivial summaries; research first | `tier-free` |
| Routine implementation with tests, and `/tdd` | `tier-go-cheap` |
| Research when the free tier cannot answer | `tier-vibe` |
| Review, critique and `/code-review` | `tier-review` |
| Open-weight work above `tier-go-cheap`; an escalation | `tier-strong-open` (refused with `tier-key-missing` until a key is provisioned) |

Move up one tier only with a reason you can write down, and never to a costlier ceiling than the task needs. The
free tiers are public-data only: the data policy keeps a repository's code off them, so a private task goes to
`tier-vibe` or `tier-go-cheap`. The run writes its routing record on its ledger line (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`, else `~/.local/state/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`; the line whose `run_id` is the run's): `tier`, `harness`, `served_model`, `attempted_fallbacks`, `fallback_fired`, `cost_usd` and `routing_capture`. Read that line instead of guessing, and say in your report which tier ran, which model served it and whether a fallback fired. A `null` field is reported as unknown, with the `routing_capture` that explains it.
Claude Code stays the interactive author and the orchestrator that rules on findings. Never send Claude traffic through LiteLLM: a tier run takes no `client`, `model`, `profile` or `harness`, so it cannot name a Claude model.

=== skills/workstation/research/SKILL.md ## Model tier
## Model tier

Research reads public pages, so it runs on tiers and never on a model picked by hand: first `tier-free`, then
`tier-vibe` for what the free tier could not answer or when it is refused or out of quota. The Gold Band
Workflow A fetchers name the same two tiers; a direct delegation runs
`model-gateway run --tier tier-free --json '{...}'`, then the same with `tier-vibe`. A repository's private
content never goes to the free tier (the gateway's data policy refuses it). The run writes its routing record on its ledger line (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`, else `~/.local/state/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`; the line whose `run_id` is the run's): `tier`, `harness`, `served_model`, `attempted_fallbacks`, `fallback_fired`, `cost_usd` and `routing_capture`. Read that line instead of guessing, and say in your report which tier ran, which model served it and whether a fallback fired. A `null` field is reported as unknown, with the `routing_capture` that explains it.
Claude Code stays the interactive author and the orchestrator that rules on findings. Never send Claude traffic through LiteLLM: a tier run takes no `client`, `model`, `profile` or `harness`, so it cannot name a Claude model.

=== skills/workstation/critique/SKILL.md ## Model tier
## Model tier

The critique stages run on the tier `tier-review`: a stage skill names it and never a model. The runner's
critics keep their `routing.json` clients, because each stage needs critics of other families than the
writer's, and a tier hides the family. Work a stage skill hands off beyond the runner runs as
`model-gateway run --tier tier-review`. The run writes its routing record on its ledger line (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`, else `~/.local/state/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`; the line whose `run_id` is the run's): `tier`, `harness`, `served_model`, `attempted_fallbacks`, `fallback_fired`, `cost_usd` and `routing_capture`. Read that line instead of guessing, and say in your report which tier ran, which model served it and whether a fallback fired. A `null` field is reported as unknown, with the `routing_capture` that explains it.
Claude Code stays the interactive author and the orchestrator that rules on findings. Never send Claude traffic through LiteLLM: a tier run takes no `client`, `model`, `profile` or `harness`, so it cannot name a Claude model.

=== skills/workstation/critique-adrs/SKILL.md ## Model tier
## Model tier

This stage's model class is the tier `tier-review`; you never pick a model by hand. The critics the runner
launches are routed by `routing.json` in the critique directory, which keeps one family per critic. Any other
model work you hand off for this stage, such as a second read or a check of one finding, runs as
`model-gateway run --tier tier-review --json '{...}'` (read-only, with `repo` so the data policy applies). The tier's Pi client reaches public data only (`data_scope: public`), so for a private repository the gateway refuses the run with its policy reason: report that refusal, and do not retry on a model you pick yourself. The run writes its routing record on its ledger line (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`, else `~/.local/state/agent-kit/model-gateway/ledger.jsonl`; the line whose `run_id` is the run's): `tier`, `harness`, `served_model`, `attempted_fallbacks`, `fallback_fired`, `cost_usd` and `routing_capture`. Read that line instead of guessing, and say in your report which tier ran, which model served it and whether a fallback fired. A `null` field is reported as unknown, with the `routing_capture` that explains it.
Claude Code stays the interactive author and the orchestrator that rules on findings. Never send Claude traffic through LiteLLM: a tier run takes no `client`, `model`, `profile` or `harness`, so it cannot name a Claude model.

